    "result_text": "The change is complete and all tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule: **a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server date is rejected with 400**, following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions.\n\nEach piece lives in its own single-responsibility unit, mirroring the existing `ValidateOwnerPostcode` / `InvalidPostcodeException` / handler trio:\n\n1. **`ValidateRegistrationDate.java`** \u2014 a validation step that reads the supplied `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, and if `registrationDate` is present and `isAfter(LocalDate.now())`, throws. An absent date passes through (defaulting happens later in `DefaultRegistrationDate`). The check is on the *supplied* value, before any business-day roll, so a future date can't slip past.\n\n2. **`FutureRegistrationDateException.java`** \u2014 a checked exception carrying the rejected date and server date for the message.\n\n3. **`FutureRegistrationDateExceptionHandler.java`** \u2014 responds `400` with a `ProblemDetail`, reusing the shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper.\n\n4. **Escalation YAML** mapping the exception to the handler.\n\n5. **Wired the step into `owners.POST.yml`** right after `validatePostcode` (alongside the other DTO validators, before defaulting/rolling), keeping it under the same `transaction` governance.\n\nTest results:\n- `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsFutureDate` (registrationDate `2999-01-01` \u2192 400): **passing**\n- Full suite: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test run, 0 failures.",
